 tract  which can improve regularity now second  we've got protein  which is comprised of strings of amino  acids which need to be broken apart in  order to be  absorbed and that does take some time  so there is a bit more work involved to  get the job done  and then last we've got fats most of the  digestion of fats occurs in the small  intestine  so they are a slow moving macronutrient  that takes your body a good amount of  work to break down  and therefore are very saturating to eat  so knowing this hierarchy of  macronutrients and digestion  we can make some inferences when it  comes to the relative  transit times of certain kinds of foods  so  meat and fish for example have protein  and fat but  no fiber so they take generally the  lungs to digest  fruit and veggies often have fiber but  in varying amounts  and in the case of something like  avocado also may have  fats the speed of their digestion  also really depends on how it's prepared  so cooked or blended kale will be  digested  much faster than raw whole kale because  these  processing techniques help to pre-digest  or break the fibers down the digestion  of starches and  other carbs will also depend on the  fiber  where things like candy will be digested  and absorbed almost immediately  but something like oatmeal or bran will  take  longer because of the extra fiber so we  really can't make blanket statements  about  how fast food groups are digested  since this will really depend on the  specific food  how it's prepared and also the gut  and metabolism of the individual who is  consuming it  now that we've discussed the alleged  digestive times of certain foods  let's tackle the whole breakfast versus  lunch versus dinner debate  when it comes to eating specific kinds  of foods  now the most genuine piece of  information here is that some people  might find that eating too close to bed  may exacerbate digestive symptoms like  acid reflux  or gerd now research suggests that a  shorter dinner to bed time  is associated with increased reflux  likely because  you're simply not allowing gravity  enough time  to help keep digestive juices in your  stomach  before you lie down and the result of  that  for some people can be acid reflux  now for everybody else who aren't  necessarily impacted by night eating  there is actually also evidence to  suggest that your basal  metabolic rate while sleeping is  virtually  the same as it is when you're awake so  in other words if the old diet culture  rhetoric about  stop eating after six has any truth to  it at all  it's not because your metabolism just  immediately shuts down when the clock  strikes  a certain time your body isn't  cinderella  but rather it's because cutting yourself  off  with some arbitrary rule means less food  is generally consumed it's also  important to note that you aren't  necessarily putting stress on your body  by consuming slower digesting foods in  the morning  and these foods certainly do not need to  be limited to just  lunch and finally the whole lunch is the  best time to eat foods of various  digestion times  is total food combining garbage your  body is  super sophisticated and can employ the  right enzymes  all at the same time to do their job  simultaneously  you don't have to worry about creating a  traffic jam  in your gut but you already know i've  already smashed that myth both here  and here so to summarize this hot mess  of the photo  there is truth to the fact that various  foods take differing amounts of time to  digest  and it can be useful to keep this  information in mind  for certain scenarios so for example  what you might want to eat before a hit  cardio workout to get fast energy  might be different than what you might  want to eat before a long meeting  that you know you can't escape from to  have a snack  having said that digestive transit times  are a  highly individualized and complex  process  and every body uses a whole host of  different factors  to determine how long i