iduals actually helps  so to summarize these claims with a few  overarching  takeaways about research in general  first  almost all of the evidence i just cited  is weak evidence that drinking water out  of a copper cup  does anything at all these claims are  largely based on some of the known  biochemical roles of copper in the body  or in a petri dish and not on any  evidence that's supplementing with it  never mind somehow kind of getting it  out of a medium  like water that touches a copper cup  makes  any clinical significant difference and  if it does  that difference would likely only be  seen for people who are  actually severely deficient but copper  deficiency is  really uncommon in humans because copper  is  readily accessible through the diet in  foods like shellfish and fish  nuts and seeds organ meats wheat bran  cereals  whole grain products and dark chocolate  groups who are  more at risk of copper deficiency  include people with celiac disease due  to  malabsorption people taking really high  doses of  zinc supplements which can interfere  with copper absorption  as well as people who are suffering from  menkin's disease  which is a very rare condition  if you are healthy and consuming a  generally well-balanced diet  you're probably meeting your copper  needs  if not is drinking from a copper cup  even a good way to get  dietary copper maybe it's  really hard to know studies have shown  that copper  is able to leach into water via copper  pipes  fixtures and alloys in water  distribution systems  as well as in household plumbing so it  is likely that it can also  do so through a cup as well how much  we don't really know so then the  question is  is it dangerous to drink your water out  of a copper cup  meh probably not while chronic exposure  to high levels of copper  can result in liver damage and gi  symptoms like abdominal pain  cramps nausea vomiting and diarrhea  copper toxicity is quite rare for the  average person who  doesn't have a hereditary copper  homeostasis defect like wilson's disease  so drink out of a copper cup because it  looks badass in hipster  and moscow mules are one of the best  adult bevies  but unless you are legit deficient it's  not likely going to make  any kind of clinically significant  difference in  any of these proposed areas of health i  think if you can take  anything away from this video it's to  highlight how  inadequate a meme is at explaining the  complexities of nutrition research  not to mention that a lot of what you  see on instagram  is just plain false and created by  people who  probably know very little to nothing  about nutrition  or how to interpret nutrition research  let's  also remember that just because you read  something on the internet that has  supposedly worked for somebody else  and their body or in the case of a lot  of these beams  worked in a vacuum or petri dish it does  not necessarily mean it's going to work  for you  in your unique environment and body i'm  not saying  everything online is bs but make sure  you're accessing information from a  reputable source  if you plan to take any ounce of it as  truth  and that is all for today folks let me  know if you guys  like this nutrition meme video by giving  it the thumbs up  also let me know below if you want to  see more of them  there is sure as hell a lot more where  that came from so  if you're on instagram make sure that  you're following me at abby's kitchen  and feel free to slide into my dms with  whatever  bunkery you come across on your feed  finally don't forget to subscribe to the  channel  and ring that little bell so you don't  miss out and i'll see you next time on  abby's kitchen  bye

